{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqpe7ptmz9g","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"Windows hook portability fix merged to main","summary":"The fix for the Windows daemon-respawn storm is MERGED to main on 2026-06-22 (merge commit 667c9808, attempt branch 3b4ebdf4-11d5-4602-bc90-2557ed1064bb-1). It adds lib/proc-list.js (cross-platform process enumeration — ps on POSIX, PowerShell Get-CimInstance on Windows, no wmic; injectable execFileSync for tests), refactors lib/headless-ancestor.js onto it (that file was genuinely ps-broken on Windows), and hardens hooks/start-daemon.js hasDaemonProcess() with a cross-platform TCP port-bind (EADDRINUSE) check that detects a daemon holding port 8787 during its load window — the exact window that triggered the respawn storm. 27 unit tests pass on Windows. This satisfies the 'keep drains off until the hook fix lands' gate from the incident note [[windows-daemon-respawn-storm-ps-hook-bug-and-detached-start-workaround]] (note-mqpd82jw9j3): the storm is now PREVENTED cross-platform. STILL REMAINING before re-enabling headless drains and returning to a hook-managed (reboot-surviving) daemon: compact the 917-node / 5965-edge graph, whose slow synchronous load is the resource-heavy vulnerable window. The live daemon is still the hand-started drains-off process.","category":null,"tags":[],"created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-22T15:51:19.930Z","vec":[-0.11723578721284866,0.00421631196513772,-0.030759060755372047,0.0501517690718174,0.09040343016386032,-0.006763269193470478,0.015872707590460777,0.013644441962242126,-0.01794794388115406,0.05934744328260422,-0.041117679327726364,0.03956202417612076,-0.009832537733018398,0.014566821046173573,-0.0027153014671057463,0.07958968728780746,0.02198699675500393,-0.0773400366306305,-0.04580721631646156,0.050147753208875656,-0.08412405103445053,0.013821830041706562,-0.031074557453393936,0.003544106148183346,-0.02361702360212803,-0.0011845427798107266,-0.10350030660629272,-0.009827254340052605,-0.041737